license: Apache-2.0

abstract: >
  Lean 4 formalization of identity-regime theory for Structural Explainability.
  The theory investigates identity criteria, transformation classifications,
  and the identity relations generated by admissible transformation steps.
  It retains a stipulated classification matrix for nine candidate identity
  profiles and ten transformation families, while separately deriving
  classifications from invariant-defined identity bases and effect models.
  The initial derived matrix contains six proof-certified cells, each with
  an explicit model, a minimally supported invariant basis, and a verified
  classification verdict. Scope decomposition, rule refinement, and forking
  demonstrate how classifications depend on field granularity, admissible
  effects, and explicit warrants. The theory also establishes conditions
  for equality and separation of step-generated identity relations.
  These results are mathematical consequences of declared models and
  assumptions. They do not establish the scientific adequacy, necessity,
  or completeness of the proposed identity criteria or regime taxonomy.

